{
  "summary": "One finding needs your attention right away: a startup shortcut named like OneDrive actually runs PowerShell hidden from view with a scrambled (encoded) command, which is a pattern malware often uses. The other finding is a low-priority reminder to confirm you recognize an administrator account on this computer. Look at the startup shortcut first, then check the account.",
  "groups": [
    {
      "priority": 1,
      "title": "Startup shortcut named 'OneDrive' runs a hidden, encoded PowerShell command",
      "finding_ids": [
        "PERSIST-SUSPICIOUS:3b484bd3"
      ],
      "explanation": "A shortcut called OneDrive.lnk sits in your personal Startup folder, so it runs every time you sign in. It does not point to OneDrive. It points to PowerShell, a built-in Windows tool that can run scripts. Its settings hide the window so you never see it run, and the command is encoded so it can't be read at a glance. Normal OneDrive startup does not work this way. Using a familiar name to disguise a hidden, encoded command is a common malware trick. The scan can't tell for certain what the command does, but this deserves prompt attention. If you or someone you trust didn't set it up on purpose, treat it as a possible infection. See the fix shown in the report for this finding."
    },
    {
      "priority": 2,
      "title": "Confirm you recognize this administrator account",
      "finding_ids": [
        "ACCT-EXTRA-ADMIN:fde0b291"
      ],
      "explanation": "There is a local account, <computer>\\<account-2>, with full control over the computer. This is often normal. Many people keep a separate administrator account on purpose, and the scan did not flag it as newly added. Just make sure you know who it belongs to and why it exists. Given the suspicious startup entry above, it's worth checking this account carefully rather than skipping it. The report shows what to do if you don't recognize it."
    }
  ],
  "severity_notes": []
}
